WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.293
Idea. In a class of thirty, six students walk to school. Six out of thirty is one fifth, which is twenty per cent. That is a percentage share.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:19.158
For unemployment, the whole is the labour force: everyone in work, plus everyone looking for work. Retired people and young children are not in it.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:26.205
So the unemployment rate is the number unemployed, divided by the labour force, times one hundred.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:38.505
Method. Step one, identify the part and the whole. Here the labour force is fifty thousand, and forty-seven thousand are employed. We are not given the unemployed.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:52.070
Step two, subtract: three thousand unemployed. Step three, divide by the labour force and times by one hundred: six per cent. Step four, round as asked and add the per cent sign.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:11.277
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2023. The Netherlands had a labour force of nine million two hundred and ten thousand, and two hundred and fifty-one thousand unemployed. Calculate the rate of unemployment, to two decimal places. Two marks.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:20.916
The part is the unemployed: two hundred and fifty-one thousand. The whole is the labour force: nine million two hundred and ten thousand.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:39.869
Two hundred and fifty-one thousand divided by nine million two hundred and ten thousand, times one hundred, is two point seven two five. To two decimal places, two point seven three per cent. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.133
So fewer than three in every hundred people in the labour force were unemployed.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:54.026
Common mistake. A common mistake is dividing by the whole population. The rate uses the labour force only.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.441
Another is forgetting to times by one hundred, or leaving off the per cent sign.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:16.747
Your turn. Now you try. Country R has a labour force of four million eight hundred thousand. Four million five hundred and sixty thousand are employed. Calculate the unemployment rate, to two decimal places. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:34.187
Answer. Unemployed is four million eight hundred thousand minus four million five hundred and sixty thousand: two hundred and forty thousand. Divide by the labour force and times by one hundred: five. The rate is five point zero zero per cent.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.747
Five in every hundred people in the labour force are unemployed.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:48.854
Remember: find the part and the whole, divide, times by one hundred, then round and add the per cent sign. Now try the practice questions.
